.Dd September 2, 2026
.Dt DIGITWM 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm digitwm
.Nd ribbon window manager for macOS
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl hkNnv
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
arranges the windows of other applications into an endless row of columns \-
the ribbon \- of which the screen shows a stretch.
It has no window of its own, no Dock icon and no menu bar.
.Pp
On macOS it moves windows through the Accessibility API, which the system
will not let it use until a person allows it.
See
.Sx THE PERMISSION ,
which is where nearly every first run goes wrong.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar file
Read
.Ar file
instead of the one the search would find.
A file named here that does not exist is an error, not a reason to look
elsewhere: somebody meant that file.
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl k
Print the key table and the command each combination runs, then exit.
Needs no permission.
.It Fl N
Go down the Apple calls this port makes, one at a time, and say which
answered.
Before the first call it prints three facts about the process itself \- which
file is running, who started it and what its signature is \- and those need no
permission either, which is the point: they are the three reasons a granted
permission does not arrive.
Exits non-zero when anything did not answer.
.It Fl n
Read the configuration, say what was made of it, and exit.
Prints the file that was read, how many directives it held, how many were
taken, how many belong to X11 alone and how many were not understood.
Needs no permission.
.It Fl v
Say out loud, on standard error, what happens: each key press that arrives,
which binding it matched, which command ran, which window held the keyboard
before and after, and the full state of the ribbon \- every display with its
origin, every ribbon with its column count, every window with the ribbon it
landed on.
.Ev DIGITWM_TRACE
set to anything does the same for a
.Nm
started by
.Xr launchd 8
or from the Finder, where there is nowhere to put a flag.
.El
.Sh THE PERMISSION
.Nm
moves windows that belong to other applications, so macOS refuses it
everything until it is allowed in
.Pa System Settings > Privacy & Security > Accessibility .
.Pp
Granting it to
.Nm
is often not enough, and this is the part that surprises people.
Three things get in the way, and
.Nm Fl N
names which one is yours before it tries anything:
.Bl -enum
.It
.Em Started from a terminal .
macOS attributes the permission not to the program but to what Apple calls the
.Em responsible code ,
and for a tool run from a shell that is the terminal application.
Grant Accessibility to the terminal
.Pq Qq Terminal , Qq iTerm , Qq Ghostty
and quit it completely \- Command-Q, not just the tab \- then start it again.
.It
.Em Installed by brew .
The name on
.Ev PATH
is a symbolic link;
.Pa /opt/homebrew/bin/digitwm
points into the Cellar.
The permission is remembered against the file the link points at.
.Nm Fl N
prints both paths and says when they differ.
.It
.Em Rebuilt since the permission was given .
A build signed ad-hoc is identified by a hash of its own contents, so a
rebuilt
.Nm
is a different program as far as the system is concerned and is asked about
again.
.Nm Fl N
prints that hash.
A stable signing identity fixes it:
.Dl make -C macos SIGNID="your certificate"
.El
.Pp
A
.Nm
started from its own application bundle is its own responsible code and gets
its own row in the Accessibility list, which removes the first of the three.
It does not remove the third: the bundle identifier does not stand in for the
signature.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Nm
reads one file, and looks for it in this order:
.Bl -enum -compact
.It
the file named with
.Fl c ;
.It
.Ev DIGITWMRC ,
when set and not empty;
.It
.Pa ~/.digitable/digitwm/digitwmrc ;
.It
.Pa ~/.cwmrc ,
the name
.Xr cwm 1
reads, kept so that a configuration written for cwm keeps working.
.Nm
says out loud, once, when this is the file it settled on.
.El
.Pp
On the first run that finds neither of the last two,
.Nm
writes
.Pa ~/.digitable/digitwm/digitwmrc
itself \- the directory 0755, the file 0644 \- containing every setting there
is, commented out, with a line of prose above each.
A file of comments changes no behaviour; it exists so that a person who opens
it can see what there is to turn.
It is written once and never rewritten: what is put in it survives every
upgrade.
.Pp
When
.Pa ~/.cwmrc
exists and ours does not, nothing is written, and the reason is printed.
Ours would win the search and hide a file that is not empty, which is exactly
the kind of silent replacement a settings file must never do.
The message names the two commands that move the old file over.
.Pp
The directives themselves are
.Xr cwmrc 5 .
Those macOS cannot honour are read and counted, not obeyed:
.Ic borderwidth
and the colours, because one program may not draw on another program's
window; groups, because Spaces has no public interface; and
.Ic ribbonwarp ,
because the public API has no call that moves the pointer.
.Nm Fl n
counts them under
.Qq X11's alone .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width DIGITWM_TRACE
.It Ev DIGITWMRC
The configuration file to read.
Set but missing is an error, as with
.Fl c .
.It Ev DIGITWM_TRACE
Set to anything: the same as
.Fl v .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ~/.digitable/digitwm/digitwmrc -compact
.It Pa ~/.digitable/digitwm/digitwmrc
The configuration.
Written on first run when absent.
.It Pa ~/.cwmrc
The name
.Xr cwm 1
uses; read when ours is absent.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Fl n
exits 1 when the configuration held a line that was not understood, and
.Fl N
exits 1 when any Apple call did not answer.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Find out what the key table is before pressing anything:
.Dl $ digitwm -k
.Pp
Find out what was made of the configuration:
.Dl $ digitwm -n
.Pp
Find out why a granted permission has not arrived:
.Dl $ digitwm -N
.Pp
Find out where a key press went:
.Dl $ digitwm -v 2>/tmp/digitwm.log
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cwm 1 ,
.Xr cwmrc 5
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
is a fork of
.Xr cwm 1 ,
the OpenBSD window manager, with a scrollable-tiling layer added and a macOS
port built on the Accessibility API and the Carbon Event Manager.
The macOS target was first built and run on a real Mac on 2 September 2026.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Digitable Aq Mt https://digitable.life ,
over
.Xr cwm 1
by the OpenBSD project.
.Sh CAVEATS
Six things macOS does not allow, and none of them is a temporary state of the
work:
a window appears where the application put it and only then moves into its
column, because macOS has no equivalent of the X11
.Dv MapRequest ;
.Ic ribbonhide
leaves a one-pixel strip, because a window cannot be pushed off the screen
entirely;
there are no groups, because Spaces has no public interface;
a full-screen window is not on the ribbon, because it lives in its own Space;
native tabs are one window to the Accessibility API and several to you;
and the focused window gets no border.
.Pp
The pointer is never moved to follow the focus, and the focus never follows
the pointer.
The public API has no call for the first, and the second without the first
oscillates.
